  <h2>Working in an existing payment system</h2>
  <p>I worked on backend and payment systems inside a large, established codebase. The value of a change in that setting comes from understanding the behaviour that customers and adjacent teams already rely on, then fitting the improvement into that wider system.</p>
  <p>The work called for focused changes rather than treating the platform as a blank slate. Existing system boundaries, payment flows and the people responsible for neighbouring areas all shaped the engineering approach.</p>

  <h2>Finding an unused payment insurance flow</h2>
  <p>I found and implemented an unused payment insurance flow in the legacy codebase. It was a concrete example of looking for a valuable improvement within existing behaviour, then carrying that work through implementation.</p>

  <CaseStudyResult
    label="Commercial contribution"
    value="Contributed nearly $5M in additional revenue through a payment insurance flow."
    detail="This describes my contribution through that flow, within a wider product and team."
  />

  <h2>Payment-provider integrations and updates</h2>
  <p>I built payment-provider integrations using .NET Web API and gRPC, alongside an automated payment method update platform. The work focused on payment-related functionality within a wider system, with the care that established customer journeys require.</p>

  <h2>Moving business logic out of SQL Server</h2>
  <p>Part of my work was moving business logic out of legacy SQL Server code and into backend services. That was a practical way to improve where the logic lived while respecting the surrounding system rather than claiming a complete platform rewrite.</p>

  <h2>Supporting the team</h2>
  <p>Alongside the payment and backend work, I mentored three junior developers. It was part of contributing to the team around the system as well as to the code itself.</p>

  <aside class="article-callout">
